#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "4bd17973-6b8a-51d6-b4c2-fb941cc1f24d" :question/math-academy-id "e-537" :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=6+9=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(2) \\approx 15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175729 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "344b786f-0c6c-5ea4-9493-c787df2543ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-7701" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\  & =40 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.  \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\Delta y = 20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "29a57695-57ff-566d-8231-bab1bc99a240" :question/math-academy-id "e-9354" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={2}^{3}-{1}^{2}=7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+7=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)\\approx 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caba7343-1eb6-54d3-919b-3dd3969a73c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313763} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4184fa-3287-5893-8a96-7aa262b33278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313764} {:answer/id #uuid "5ceedacf-7e4e-5073-94c0-345c4ed26583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313765} {:answer/id #uuid "164348c7-2dd4-58f4-981d-a22e1c2790d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313766} {:answer/id #uuid "df96c533-7f1d-55c6-91fe-9f2708e02afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c4184fa-3287-5893-8a96-7aa262b33278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313764} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed466e7b-ddf1-5f50-9ff2-646c178ee31f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3cedab7-42aa-56ef-928f-3cf1df645c6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-117941" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y-2x,\\,y(2)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=2$ to $x=4$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7503171-48c1-572a-b778-a96dc87d931d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313770} {:answer/id #uuid "650d7df1-c3b4-5023-bbb2-6a82985a2eca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313771} {:answer/id #uuid "9eff65a7-6617-5ea9-ad8c-130eab998350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313772} {:answer/id #uuid "009b58b1-6139-582d-aea6-e2303576b996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313773} {:answer/id #uuid "10539ea7-cfbb-5a81-9213-f1c149802af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009b58b1-6139-582d-aea6-e2303576b996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313773} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d1ffbe3-18d3-5162-907b-c7c9cc10409e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93e335a7-7c6a-5a30-a6e8-7fdd11e7900a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117954" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(2)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(5).$ The distance from $x=2$ and $x=5$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y=2+2\\cdot (-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y=3+2\\cdot (-1)=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y=4+2\\cdot 0=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+1=5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+4=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $5$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab2ac0fc-79e1-5a62-83b0-2ca5f3f04743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297977} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6f6249-c6f4-5d6e-a38a-3cd70ea0f22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297978} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac3239d-0149-501a-a0d5-f27013991428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297979} {:answer/id #uuid "29ee4c43-6b3f-53fa-97d1-2a7c08a40bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297980} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbf82a0-a6b6-5e36-a5e9-4ad812af04c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29ee4c43-6b3f-53fa-97d1-2a7c08a40bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297980} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f295d12-946c-54f9-a31f-23e3520f8928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae67b5f0-7d38-5916-8b7c-e399d74aab69" :question/math-academy-id "q-117968" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2},\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+0=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c66b7dbe-4d44-5cca-a354-64669e898024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313777} {:answer/id #uuid "3f986cf4-813a-5c42-806b-5cbd1a080a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313778} {:answer/id #uuid "caac6ad5-1441-5cc0-a19e-2207ed2ef541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313779} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f9d3f4-8663-5b05-8ce6-aa0badc3de20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313780} {:answer/id #uuid "498d78d4-c34d-5fc6-bba0-0f2d99322bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caac6ad5-1441-5cc0-a19e-2207ed2ef541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313779} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d68863-c5fb-5925-b98e-08df52ffbe7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f77be12-c79a-5d32-a315-5527f2d659be" :question/math-academy-id "q-117969" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(y-1),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=1\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=3\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aa61118-f2cc-5710-8b5d-d3b48cfe28b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313784} {:answer/id #uuid "296dc199-3813-53c0-aed5-011aa4c98350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313785} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7f50da-3cda-5c2f-b7cf-674df7bd88bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313786} {:answer/id #uuid "1e50adbc-ce7f-5977-aff7-5358d428f4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313787} {:answer/id #uuid "4febd929-13b2-524c-adef-b9bd83d42b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "296dc199-3813-53c0-aed5-011aa4c98350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313785} :answer-field/id #uuid "458a208c-28cd-5aef-accb-36c35531326c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64160470-ada8-5122-8c04-a58ec889ddc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-120842" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot e \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74690141-04cc-5a4e-b855-0adfdbc1b61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298005} {:answer/id #uuid "c136aa62-560a-5f39-8d16-89d554dc8dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74690141-04cc-5a4e-b855-0adfdbc1b61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298005} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c1ae38-5640-5e62-b35b-9835cec1cf71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54be50ac-2279-5ba6-a1f2-5576a1ca045e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348069" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf808b13-03af-53d7-a45d-14aefc7038f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf808b13-03af-53d7-a45d-14aefc7038f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313791} :answer-field/id #uuid "c511e5bc-6bef-5265-9fb4-1ad67377b66d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1cefd4a-1586-50ea-8f31-7e2d90aafc4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-351667" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y},\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(1)≈\\frac{17}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32679d15-f334-5be2-b459-23cdf1e1aff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32679d15-f334-5be2-b459-23cdf1e1aff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313794} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d62432b-8f0c-543c-886d-76ba33a0c567" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0234c10a-63c8-5c42-9892-19c8b4b18813" :question/math-academy-id "q-351668" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+x)^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+x)^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ce50f2-2e13-505c-9061-5cddecd3b650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ce50f2-2e13-505c-9061-5cddecd3b650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313797} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e19446c-b0c6-5597-bd8e-6fa28231d437" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f182d6d-9c83-56c4-bc5d-2dcffc2121f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-351690" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+5-y,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=-1+5-2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=0+5-4=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=1+5-5=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+1=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7487a604-95e3-5261-aec3-098f33a91bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7487a604-95e3-5261-aec3-098f33a91bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313800} :answer-field/id #uuid "6838e483-7820-5f22-b04e-6d0e1b0169b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bf69a41-14a3-5531-887a-c43b607457a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-351692" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x+2y,\\,y(2)=-3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(5).$\n\n$y(5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(5).$ The distance from $x=2$ and $x=5$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=-3.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(2)+2(-3)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(3)+2(-1)=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+10=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(4)+2(9)=34 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=34\\cdot 1=34 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+1=5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=9+34=43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $34$ | $34$ |\n| $5$ | $43$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈43.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a625a232-8ffe-50aa-a03e-3335fc56f5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313803} {:answer/id #uuid "1213780a-bcb5-53bb-8366-13f91af93f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313804} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd1ca90-979e-5373-9be3-1f0d7b081f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313805} {:answer/id #uuid "333b4d8e-1b1c-5bea-b6f7-cc51451a144a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313806} {:answer/id #uuid "1f09c1f7-235b-5c77-83bb-ec6f59559eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a625a232-8ffe-50aa-a03e-3335fc56f5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313803} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ca2cd7-7f6e-555d-8826-70253ab0945f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d3d9541-c4d2-58db-87f5-eff80c4fcbe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-50091" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+10=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 12=52 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=52\\cdot 1=52 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+52=64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ | $52$ | $52$ |\n| $3$ | $64$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈64.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a1b3ef-a147-5017-86db-ae13d3616125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313810} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdd75aa-971c-554a-a4f5-bffc90dea1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313811} {:answer/id #uuid "4468d990-b506-526f-b8d2-e3701388d6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313812} {:answer/id #uuid "a27a4348-b482-542b-90d5-db70370d9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313813} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb292-6c15-5813-86e9-14c0376c444f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27a4348-b482-542b-90d5-db70370d9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b36b11-1994-515a-90ae-54a5da40dd35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5237bd2c-76dc-568c-82b6-a0490e966574" :question/math-academy-id "q-53864" :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(3)=2,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=3$ to $x=4$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be  \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(3)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=3+2=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{9}{2}+4=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈\\frac{17}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98e39fa9-6518-588f-8af3-6a0eeb591b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175219} {:answer/id #uuid "d17589d6-c66b-5df0-9b44-267c44fc80e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175220} {:answer/id #uuid "de106bea-e2cf-56ce-bf97-c8de4a2cf624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175221} {:answer/id #uuid "477334da-4452-5884-bee3-ea98b0a57519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175222} {:answer/id #uuid "6afaf48c-0c2b-5d64-b637-f6f3acf741a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de106bea-e2cf-56ce-bf97-c8de4a2cf624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175221} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2e5883-7f33-5001-b6a3-9c835b8ae68e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3212ad75-6143-5882-84d1-88a833797bb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-53876" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={1}^{2}\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={2}^{2}\\cdot 2=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36819efe-53f1-5361-90d9-402c1c19d622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313817} {:answer/id #uuid "c0226456-8ba8-5fdf-8c25-82622d1c74b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313818} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fe6b7f-9b0c-5393-b012-a91d6aada416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313819} {:answer/id #uuid "37ca932c-eaf8-57f0-8156-3ffb74e55a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313820} {:answer/id #uuid "21d25e5f-2cd0-5297-adb3-a5b09cb0eb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21d25e5f-2cd0-5297-adb3-a5b09cb0eb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313821} :answer-field/id #uuid "634b61cc-041c-5c4c-a19e-e95d278a5cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14f5a6a1-04ba-5222-a3fd-717ecd4bf55b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86901" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =12 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $12$ | $24$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e46038-34a7-5c27-bbb7-6008335450f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(e+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313824} {:answer/id #uuid "b72ed886-d81e-5c2d-a79e-de3bd13835a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313825} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d67b05-0a9a-5fa5-b645-773ff1ba92ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313826} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcdfa00-091f-5a68-a5d8-3105e9882e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(e+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313827} {:answer/id #uuid "2b49a550-4f7b-54e6-b763-69ba57cbdca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d67b05-0a9a-5fa5-b645-773ff1ba92ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313826} :answer-field/id #uuid "1662cab7-77c7-57e3-a4bd-2de2b3e9f5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8abd32b-d6f2-52ef-8053-9fe190d5f3da" :question/math-academy-id "q-89084" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(2)=e\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =e+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ | $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ | $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ |"}]}
